{"eligibility":"include","exclusion_criterion":"","side_list":"none","eligibility_reason":"An off-the-shelf LLM iteratively proposes loop-transformation schedules that are applied to existing PolyBench code, guided by compiler legality and measured-speedup feedback. Runtime speedup of the resulting optimized code is reported. This is borderline because the LLM emits transformation commands and the compiler generates the code; an ablation (RQ7) has the LLM rewrite the C code directly.","title":"Agentic Auto-Scheduling: An Experimental Study of LLM-Guided Loop Optimization","year":2025,"venue":"2025 34th International Conference on Parallel Architectures and Compilation Techniques (PACT)","venue_type":"peer-reviewed-conf","study_type":"technique-proposal","techniques":[{"technique_name":"ComPilot (LLM-compiler closed-loop schedule optimization)","family_v0":"feedback-loop","stage":"post-generation-loop","signal_source":["compiler","profiler"],"tools":"Tiramisu compiler (commit 041afad) with polyhedral dependence analysis; runtime measurement on 2x Intel Xeon E5-2695 v2","iterations":"T=30 iterations (distinct schedules) per run; LLM may issue no_further_transformations but is pushed to continue up to the iteration limit; best-of-K=5 runs","dims_targeted":["efficiency"],"dims_measured":["efficiency"],"correctness_measured":true,"metrics":"efficiency: geometric mean of median speedup over the original code, Pluto and the Tiramisu autoscheduler [execution]; correctness: legality guaranteed by dependence analysis, plus runnable/invalid/illegal schedule rates; output comparison in the RQ7 direct-code-generation variant","benchmarks":"PolyBench/C 4.2.1, 30 kernels x 5 dataset sizes = 150 instances","languages":"C/C++","models":"gemini-2.0-flash (closed); gpt-4o (closed); gpt-o3-mini (closed, reasoning); gemma3 27B (open); llama3.3 70B (open, instruct); qwq 32B (open, reasoning); qwen2.5-coder 32B (open, code); codestral-2501 22B (open, code)","baselines":"original code; Pluto polyhedral optimizer; Tiramisu deep-learning autoscheduler; no-feedback ablation; LLM direct code generation variant","effect_target":"+","effect_target_headline":"geometric mean speedups of 2.66x (single run) and 3.54x (best-of-5 runs) over the original code; 2.94× over Pluto (best-of-5)","effect_correctness":"NR","effect_correctness_headline":"36.1% of the proposed schedules are successfully compiled and run (runnable), while 31.4% are invalid and 32.5% are illegal","effect_other_dims":{"readability-maintainability":"NR","efficiency":"targeted","security":"NR"}}],"compute_reported":["tokens","time"],"contamination_handled":"partial","human_eval":"none","human_eval_details":"","artifact_url":"","reimplementable":"yes","qa":{"QA1":1,"QA2":1,"QA3":1,"QA4":0.5,"QA5":1,"QA6":1,"QA7":1,"QA8":0,"QA9":0.5,"QA10":0},"confidence":"medium","notes":"Borderline on IC2/EC4: the LLM selects transformation commands rather than writing code, and the code being optimized is human-written PolyBench. Contamination is only partly addressed: identifiers are anonymized for robustness, not for contamination. There is no explicit threats-to-validity section. Some figures are garbled in the extracted text."}
```

