{"eligibility":"exclude","exclusion_criterion":"EC4","side_list":"adjacent","eligibility_reason":"PSSec (SFT+RL on lightweight Qwen3 models) is evaluated only on analysing and repairing existing human-written PowerShell scripts from The Stack, measuring static-analyzer fix success. The CodeGen part of the benchmark measures security of LLM-generated scripts without any intervention, which is context only.","title":"Lightweight Yet Secure: Secure Scripting Language Generation via Lightweight LLMs","year":2026,"venue":"preprint","venue_type":"","study_type":"","techniques":"","compute_reported":"","contamination_handled":"","human_eval":"","human_eval_details":"","artifact_url":"","reimplementable":"","qa":"","confidence":"low","notes":"Borderline case. IC2 allows code edited by an LLM, and the paper frames PSSec as a downstream fixer for code produced by a frontier LLM. However, every reported PSSec result is on repair of human-written scripts from The Stack. The NF metric is FSucRate: PSScriptAnalyzer Warning/Error-level violations removed. Many of the rules are style or readability rules rather than security rules. A reviewer should decide whether LLM repair of existing code satisfies IC2; if so, the paper should be included under security with a training-time technique."}
```

