{"eligibility":"include","exclusion_criterion":"","side_list":"none","eligibility_reason":"Evaluates zero-shot security-repair prompting of GPT-4o, GPT-4.1, GPT-5 and Claude Opus 4.1 on code from DevGPT. That code is partly GPT-generated (31 of 56 confirmed issues). Fix rates for the LLM-edited code are reported as a quantitative security outcome, including separately for GPT-generated code. Borderline: the study is largely an empirical model evaluation and mixes user-written with GPT-generated code.","title":"Secure Coding with AI – From Detection to Repair: Vulnerability Analysis and Fixing Using Large Language Models","year":2026,"venue":"Empirical Software Engineering (arXiv 2504.20814)","venue_type":"peer-reviewed-journal","study_type":"technique-evaluation","techniques":[{"technique_name":"Zero-shot security detect-and-fix prompting (senior security developer persona, asks for CWE and fixed code)","family_v0":"prompting","stage":"post-generation-loop","signal_source":["none"],"tools":"Static scanners used to identify issues for selection (not fed to LLM): Snyk 1.1294.0, Semgrep 1.87.0, Flawfinder 2.0.19, Cppcheck 2.14.0","iterations":"single-shot, 1 round","dims_targeted":["security"],"dims_measured":["security"],"correctness_measured":false,"metrics":"security: number/percent of scanner-flagged, manually confirmed vulnerabilities detected and fixed (human manual review; abstract also mentions re-scanning)","benchmarks":"DevGPT snapshots 20231012 and 20240514; C/C++/C# files; 2024 run: 26 files/32 issues; 2025 run: 48 files/56 issues (31 issues in GPT-generated code)","languages":"C, C++, C#","models":"GPT-4o (closed, 2024, temperature 1.0); GPT-4.1 (closed, temperature 0.1); GPT-5 (closed reasoning, temperature 1.0); Claude Opus 4.1 (closed, temperature 0.1)","baselines":"Original vulnerable code; comparison across models and across time (Oct 2024 vs Sep 2025)","effect_target":"+","effect_target_headline":"GPT-4.1 detected 82% of the problems and fixed 75%, GPT-5 detected and fixed 78.6%, and Claude Opus 4.1 detected 80.4% and fixed 76.8%","effect_correctness":"NR","effect_correctness_headline":"","effect_other_dims":{"readability-maintainability":"NR","efficiency":"NR","security":"targeted"}}],"compute_reported":["none"],"contamination_handled":"partial","human_eval":"rating","human_eval_details":"First author reviewed manually; findings discussed and validated by the second and third authors; no agreement statistic reported","artifact_url":"https://github.com/vlad2010/MSc_DataEngineering_Results","reimplementable":"yes","qa":{"QA1":1,"QA2":0.5,"QA3":0.5,"QA4":0,"QA5":1,"QA6":0,"QA7":0,"QA8":1,"QA9":0.5,"QA10":1},"confidence":"low","notes":"Borderline inclusion: mostly an empirical study of model capability. Code mixes user-written (25 issues) and GPT-generated (31 issues) sources; Table 18 gives results for GPT-generated code only. Fix verification is described as manual review, while Further Work says re-scanning of fixed code is planned, which conflicts with the abstract. No functional correctness check of the fixes. Single run with no variance reported. Tables are garbled."}
```

