{"eligibility":"exclude","exclusion_criterion":"EC4","side_list":"adjacent","eligibility_reason":"Based on the abstract only, GraphGlue uses a multi-agent LLM system to repair and optimize existing human-written DNN programs so they compile into full computation graphs. This is LLM optimization or repair of human code, not a technique that improves the NF quality of code an LLM generates. It is borderline, because the LLM-edited code is evaluated on efficiency.","title":"Fixing Broken Graphs: LLM-Powered Automatic Code Optimization for DNN Programs","year":2025,"venue":"International Conference on Automated Software Engineering","venue_type":"","study_type":"","techniques":"","compute_reported":"","contamination_handled":"","human_eval":"","human_eval_details":"","artifact_url":"","reimplementable":"","qa":"","confidence":"low","notes":"Only the abstract was available. The paper reports speedup (up to 2.19x, 1.23x on average, vs. TorchDynamo) and memory savings (up to 15.77x vs. AI compiler frontends) on LLM-edited code. If the review treats LLM-edited code-optimization papers as in scope under IC2, this paper should be reconsidered for inclusion with efficiency as the targeted dimension. Code: https://github.com/Jamesswang/GraphGlue"}
```

